 For all real numbers $x$ and $y$, notice $\dfrac{1}{x}+\dfrac{1}{y}=\dfrac{x+y}{xy}$.  Hence $\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}$ is equal to the right hand side of $\dfrac{a+b}{ab}$.  Using the identities $a+b=7$ and $a^3+b^3=(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2)$ we see that
$ab(a^2-ab+b^2)=(a+b)((a+b)^2-3ab)$, where $a+b=7$, so
$ab(a+b-ab)=7((a+b)^2-3ab)$, or $7ab(7-ab)=49(49-3ab)$.  From here we see the quadratic
$3ab(7-ab)-49(7-ab)=0$.  There are many ways to solve this equation, and $ab=7$ or $ab=\dfrac{49}{3}$ are the only roots, and the first gives $ab=\dfrac{(a+b)^2}{4}=49/4$.  Hence
$\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}$ is equal to either $\dfrac{a+b}{49/4}=\dfrac{4}{7}$ or $\dfrac{7}{49/3}=\dfrac{3}{7}$.

